You said:
nginx config - is the keyword "location" always followed by a path and then a "{"? And is the path expected to start with "/" and be appended to "root" within the location to make sense?
ChatGPT said:

Great questions—this stuff is confusing at first, and nginx doesn’t help by being a little… opinionated 😄
1️⃣ Is location always followed by a path and then {?

Almost always, yes.
The general shape is:

location <modifier?> <pattern> {
    ...
}

Examples:

location / {
}

location /images/ {
}

location = /favicon.ico {
}

location ~ \.php$ {
}

So:

    There is always a pattern

    It is always followed by { ... }

    The pattern is not always a “path” in the filesystem sense

There are different kinds of patterns though:
Type	Example	Meaning
Prefix (most common)	location /images/	URL path starts with /images/
Exact match	location = /login	URL path must be exactly /login
Regex	location ~ \.php$	URL path matches regex
Case-insensitive regex	location ~* \.jpg$	Same, case-insensitive
“Prefer prefix”	location ^~ /static/	Stop searching if this matches

But syntactically: yes, location … {} is mandatory.
2️⃣ Does the location “path” have to start with /?

For prefix and exact matches: yes, it must start with / because it matches the URI path of the request.

✔ Valid:

location / {
}

location /api/ {
}

location = /health {
}

❌ Invalid / meaningless:

location api {
}

For regex locations, it does not have to start with /:

location ~ \.php$ {
}

That’s because it’s matching against the URI using a regex, not a path prefix.
3️⃣ Is the location path “appended to root”?

This is the most important nuance 👇

Yes — but only when you use root.

Example:

root /var/www/site;

location /images/ {
}

Request:

/images/logo.png

nginx looks for:

/var/www/site/images/logo.png

So:

root + URI = filesystem path

If you instead define root inside the location:

location /images/ {
    root /data;
}

Then nginx looks for:

/data/images/logo.png

4️⃣ But alias breaks this mental model (on purpose)

With alias, nginx replaces the matched location instead of appending it:

location /images/ {
    alias /data/img/;
}

Request:

/images/logo.png

Maps to:

/data/img/logo.png

⚠️ Important rule:

    alias must end with a slash if the location does

    root does not have this restriction

TL;DR mental model

    location → always has a pattern and { }

    Prefix/exact locations → start with /

    Regex locations → don’t have to

    root → root + full URI

    alias → replace the matched location

